# The whole vocabulary

This section is Loom's entire syntax on one screen. Eight marks, each with its glyph, its name and a sentence saying what it does — and nothing held back for a later page.

Showing all of it at once is the argument. A reader deciding whether to try a tool wants to know how much there is to learn, and the honest answer here is short enough to print. A vocabulary you can read in a minute is a different proposition from one you have to work through.

## The eight marks

Each mark carries a colour, and the colour is the mark's own wherever it appears again — in the operations below, in the source view, in the docs. A glyph sits in a chip of its colour over a twelve-percent wash of the same, which is enough to separate it from the prose without turning the row into a swatch.

The list counts a heading once. A part is the same `#` mark one level deeper, so it earns a sentence inside the heading's row rather than a row of its own — the count is meant to be the number of things to learn, not the number of ways to write them.

=>

import { Array } from 'effect'
import { type Html, inertHtml as ih } from 'foldkit/html'
import { broadSeam, carpetMark, vocabularyCarpet } from './components'

type Mark = {
  readonly glyph: string
  readonly colour: string
  readonly tint: string
  readonly name: string
  readonly says: string
}

export const marks: ReadonlyArray<Mark> = [
  {
    glyph: '---',
    colour: '#8B5CF6',
    tint: '#F0EAFE',
    name: 'Frontmatter',
    says: 'Frontmatter opens a chapter and carries its metadata.',
  },
  {
    glyph: '#',
    colour: '#2E6FF2',
    tint: '#EAF1FE',
    name: 'Section',
    says: 'A heading opens a section and names it.',
  },
  {
    glyph: '{{ }}',
    colour: '#5A8DF6',
    tint: '#EDF3FE',
    name: 'Warp',
    says: 'Warps bind variables which are used for values interpolation.',
  },
  {
    glyph: '[ ]',
    colour: '#2E6FF2',
    tint: '#EAF1FE',
    name: 'Sink',
    says: 'A sink names the file a section tangles into.',
  },
  {
    glyph: '{ }',
    colour: '#8A5A00',
    tint: '#FDF3DF',
    name: 'Specifier',
    says: 'A specifier gives a section its language or its role.',
  },
  {
    glyph: '=>',
    colour: '#00996B',
    tint: '#E4F7F0',
    name: 'Arrow',
    says: 'An arrow turns from prose into code.',
  },
  {
    glyph: '~',
    colour: '#E8501F',
    tint: '#FDEEE8',
    name: 'Tilde',
    says: 'A tilde turns back from code into prose.',
  },
  {
    glyph: `::${'['} ]`,
    colour: '#8B5CF6',
    tint: '#F0EAFE',
    name: 'Anchor',
    says: 'An anchor transcludes the section or a warp.',
  },
]

~

The anchor's glyph is written with its bracket interpolated rather than typed. `::` followed by a bracket is how Loom opens an anchor, so typing it whole here would make this list's own source try to draw in a section called `a section`. Breaking the token is what lets the vocabulary quote the mark it is describing.

The chip's wash is the mark's own colour at an eighth strength, written as the colour with an alpha pair appended. Deriving it from the colour rather than listing a second one means a mark can never end up with a wash that does not match it.

=>

const glyphChip = (mark: Mark): Html =>
  ih.span(
    [ih.Class('mark-glyph'), ih.Style({ color: mark.colour, background: `${mark.colour}1F` })],
    [mark.glyph],
  )

## One card per mark

A mark gets a card: its glyph in a chip, its name beside the chip in the mark's own colour, and one sentence under both. Nothing is numbered. A vocabulary is a set rather than a sequence, and eight numbered rows invite a reader to think there is an order to learn them in.

The card lies quiet until the cursor is on it, and then it takes the mark's colour twice over — the ground goes to the mark's tint, the edge to the mark's own hue. That is the only way the colour reaches the card, so a reader who runs down the set sees each mark's colour arrive and leave one at a time.

=>

const markCard = (mark: Mark): Html =>
  ih.div(
    [
      ih.Class('mark-card'),
      ih.Style({ '--mark-ink': mark.colour, '--mark-tint': mark.tint }),
    ],
    [
      ih.span(
        [ih.Class('mark-card-head')],
        [glyphChip(mark), ih.span([ih.Class('mark-name')], [mark.name])],
      ),
      ih.span([ih.Class('mark-says')], [mark.says]),
    ],
  )

## The section

The section opens with the header block every section wears — the woven mark, the eyebrow, the heading — then gives the rest of its width to the vocabulary. At forty-two pixels the mark takes the broader seam, since a carpet this size shows the rounding a fine one leaves.

=>

export const vocabulary = (): Html =>
  ih.div(
    [ih.Class('section'), ih.Id('loom-marks')],
    [
      ih.div(
        [ih.Class('section-head')],
        [
          carpetMark(vocabularyCarpet, '42px', broadSeam),
          ih.div(
            [ih.Class('lines')],
            [
              ih.span([ih.Class('eyebrow')], ['Loom syntax']),
              ih.h2([], ['The whole vocabulary']),
            ],
          ),
        ],
      ),
      ih.div([ih.Class('mark-set')], Array.map(marks, markCard)),
    ],
  )

## The module {Tangle}[vocabulary.ts]

The module is the marks themselves, the chip that draws a glyph, the card that holds one, and the section that holds them all.

